cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  telemgap: separating missing telemetry from corrupt telemetry from a reader
  that is wrong, with a refusable gap policy
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-28
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Takes a decoded telemetry record with timestamps and produces an integrity
  report: gaps with their durations, duplicate and out-of-order frames, clock
  resets and backward jumps, and a classification of each anomaly into
  missing, corrupt, or correctly-received-and-misparsed, with undecided as a
  first-class fourth verdict that carries its reason. The first output of
  every call is a determinability verdict -- seven limits computed from the
  record supplied, each with a number, three of them structural and never
  determinable. A misparse is concluded only on positive evidence: an
  alternative declared reading of the same raw word that is both inside the
  channel's range and consistent with its neighbours, a pair of conditions
  that tightens the chance-rescue rate by a factor of 3.18 to 5.57 over range
  membership alone. In front of any interpolation the package emits an
  explicit, refusable policy: per gap and per channel, a Lagrange-remainder
  error bound, a coverage term for endpoint measurement noise, a Nyquist gate,
  a check that the curvature bound is measurable and resolvable at all, and a
  check that the frames bounding the gap are not themselves anomalous.
  Validation is level 2. The interpolation bound held on all 12,800
  chord-versus-truth comparisons across 32 swept cells, against both the
  analytic curvature and an independently derived Bernstein bound that shares
  no mathematics with it. Gap, duplicate and reorder recall are 1.0000 on
  every cell of a 16-cell swept grid, with zero timing false positives on
  clean records. Four results are unfavourable to this package and are
  published as measured. The declared-range screen cannot see 0.862371 of
  single-bit errors on the worst channel, enumerated over all 65,536 word
  codes, which caps the corrupt recall of every predictor here at 0.5098. The
  coverage statement failed once in sixteen channel-records, traced to a bit
  error that stayed in range and passed the frame error-control check, so no
  screen saw it and the endpoint gate could not refuse the gap it anchored. An
  estimated curvature bound under-predicts a near-Nyquist channel by a factor
  of 203, a bias rather than noise, caught only by a resolvability gate whose
  absence makes the policy exceed its prediction on 8 of 8 seeds. And on the
  full held-out population the learned random forest wins four of nine
  per-class metrics against the strengthened rule baseline -- rules 3, learned
  4, tie 2 -- which is not what the specification predicted; the rules keep
  the one large margin, surfacing 0.7647 of the corrupt class against 0.5098,
  because they abstain where they cannot decide. On the ambiguous residual set
  the learned component was scored against a majority-class guess: one
  declared configuration beats it by 10.5 accuracy points and another loses to
  it by 56.5, with the imbalance handling deciding the answer completely and
  the architecture identical between them. Fifteen controls were run, eleven
  of which must fire and eleven of which did, four of which must not and none
  of which did. All numbers are measured against a seeded synthetic telemetry
  generator whose equations are printed in its module docstring; no flight
  data is used anywhere. Research grade. Not flight-qualified, not certified,
  not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - telemetry
  - space-operations
  - data-integrity
  - gap-analysis
  - interpolation
  - ccsds
  - anomaly-classification
references:
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Hildebrand
        given-names: "F. B."
    title: "Introduction to Numerical Analysis"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "Dover"
    year: 1987
    notes: >-
      Section 3.2 for the Lagrange remainder of a first-degree interpolating
      polynomial, which is equation (3) of telemgap.policy, and for the
      first-degree Taylor remainder behind the zero-order hold bound of
      equation (4).
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Atkinson
        given-names: "K. E."
    title: "An Introduction to Numerical Analysis"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 1989
    notes: >-
      Section 3.2, the same interpolation remainder stated independently.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Boas
        given-names: "R. P."
    title: "Entire Functions"
    publisher:
      name: "Academic Press"
    year: 1954
    notes: >-
      Theorem 11.1.2, Bernstein's inequality for band-limited functions. This
      is equation (8) and it is the independent cross-check on equation (3):
      a complex-analytic bound on an entire function of exponential type,
      sharing no mathematics with a Taylor remainder on a real interval.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Shannon
        given-names: "C. E."
    title: "Communication in the presence of noise"
    journal: "Proceedings of the IRE"
    volume: 37
    issue: 1
    year: 1949
    notes: >-
      The sampling theorem behind the Nyquist gate of telemgap.policy. The
      gate's default of four cadence slots is a declared policy choice and is
      labelled as such wherever it is reported.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Savitzky
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Golay
        given-names: "M. J. E."
    title: >-
      Smoothing and differentiation of data by simplified least squares
      procedures
    journal: "Analytical Chemistry"
    volume: 36
    issue: 8
    year: 1964
    notes: >-
      The quadratic-fit second-derivative estimator of equation (9). Its
      transfer function is computed in closed form by this package and is
      0.00326383 at 0.35 of the sampling frequency, which is why its curvature
      estimate of a near-Nyquist channel is 203 times too small.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Rousseeuw
        given-names: "P. J."
      - family-names: Croux
        given-names: "C."
    title: "Alternatives to the median absolute deviation"
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: 88
    issue: 424
    year: 1993
    notes: >-
      The 1.4826 consistency constant making a scaled median absolute
      deviation an unbiased estimator of the standard deviation for Gaussian
      data, used in equations (5) and (16).
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Harris
        given-names: "F. J."
    title: >-
      On the use of windows for harmonic analysis with the discrete Fourier
      transform
    journal: "Proceedings of the IEEE"
    volume: 66
    issue: 1
    year: 1978
    notes: >-
      The Hann window in equation (17), applied because a rectangular window
      leaks a strong low-frequency term across the spectrum at a rate that
      swamps the small high-frequency component the resolvability gate is
      looking for.
  - type: standard
    title: >-
      Evaluation of measurement data -- Guide to the expression of uncertainty
      in measurement
    institution:
      name: "Joint Committee for Guides in Metrology"
    number: "JCGM 100:2008"
    year: 2008
    notes: >-
      Section 6.3, the coverage factor. This is why equation (3') is called a
      coverage statement and never a bound: its deterministic part is a
      theorem and its endpoint-noise term is not.
  - type: standard
    title: "TM Space Data Link Protocol"
    institution:
      name: "Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems"
    number: "CCSDS 132.0-B-3"
    year: 2021
    notes: >-
      Section 4.1.4.2, the Frame Error Control Field, which is what
      TelemetryRecord.validity models. No numerical value in this repository is
      taken from it.
  - type: standard
    title: "Space Packet Protocol"
    institution:
      name: "Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems"
    number: "CCSDS 133.0-B-2"
    year: 2020
    notes: >-
      Section 4.1.3.5 for the Packet Error Control Field and section 4.1 for
      the big-endian wire order that makes a missing byte swap the commonest
      decommutation fault on a little-endian ground host. The 14-bit packet
      sequence count is the counter modulus of 16384 used by
      telemgap.dataset. No numerical value in this repository is taken from it.
  - type: standard
    title: "Mission Operations -- Parameter Services"
    institution:
      name: "Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems"
    number: "CCSDS 522.1-B-1"
    year: 2021
    notes: >-
      Section 3, the affine parameter calibration that is equation (1), the
      only decommutation form this package models.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Pedregosa
        given-names: "F."
      - family-names: Varoquaux
        given-names: "G."
      - family-names: Gramfort
        given-names: "A."
    title: "Scikit-learn: machine learning in Python"
    journal: "Journal of Machine Learning Research"
    volume: 12
    year: 2011
    notes: >-
      RandomForestClassifier, whose per-member probabilities telemgap.learned
      reads to produce the ensemble dispersion it reports as an uncertainty
      output. That read is why scikit-learn is pinned below 1.10.
